SM(210,{"n":"Colossians 3.1-13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Colossians 3.1-13.mp3","d":2331.8,"s":[[0.0,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.24,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.4,"We'll be picking up here in St."],[24.4,"Paul's Epistle. to the Colossians, chapter 3, verse 1"],[29.72,"Um, as usual, I won't go through the whole introduction again because by the time people are listening to this, they will have been able to uh listen to uh the introduction on the first uh Bible study on Colossians, but uh to get us kind of caught up a little bit on uh what Saint Paul's been talking about uh tonight is that um Saint Paul, we talked about how in the in the long introduction, how there's a lot of people spend a lot of time trying to identify a particular quote unquote Colossian heresy or some group based on what's called mirror reading."],[73.06,"You look at what St."],[73.94,"Paul is preaching against, and then you say, well, okay, so they believed XYZ."],[79.46,"Um and then people tie themselves in knots to try and get XYZ to be a coherent group or an actual historical group."],[90.1,"And we talked about how really there's no reason to suspect that there's just one particular group that St."],[98.26,"Paul is talking about."],[99.22,"He doesn't ever talk."],[100.14,"Talk about it as a group."],[102.06,"He talks about different beliefs."],[105.26,"So we saw last time, for example, that he talked about both things that seem to apply mostly to them as former pagans, and sort of the pagan pursuit of wisdom and mysteries and mystery cults and that kind of thing."],[119.02,"And then at the same time, we also saw him warning against the potential arrival of certain Jewish parties who would try to get them to be circumcised and follow uh the commandments of the Torah in sort of the the old covenant sense."],[137.28,"Would try to impose those uh on Gentiles."],[141.04,"Obviously, those are not the same group."],[143.66,"So it takes a lot of special pleading to try to make them the same group."],[147.66,"Um what unites those ideas, though, uh, beyond just, you know, St."],[153.98,"Paul warning about anybody bad who might show up, is the idea, and this is an idea that as former pagans, which most of the believers in Colossae would have been, as most of the believers in Laodicea, who would have probably also received this epistle would have been, is that within within the pagan religion of the first century, there was sort of always another level or another step or a new mystery to be initiated into, or a new god, or a new cult, or a new festival, or a new set."],[190.73,"You you could sort of keep going deeper and deeper, and religion was in sort of this experiential mode of okay, now you're gonna have this new deeper experience of XYZ."],[202.97,"And so this is this is the kind of spirituality that they were used to practicing"],[210.29,"Um what unites all of these things is that these are different groups of people with different ideas that might show up and appeal to these former pagans on that level."],[225.49,"So this might be the next step, basically."],[227.56,"So the Judaizers might come and say, well, if you really want to be a follower of the God of Israel, then the next step is you need to be circumcised and you need to start following these food laws and you need to start observing uh these Jewish feast days uh and festivals in addition to the Christian observances."],[248.9,"So claiming to offer sort of more."],[251.32,"And then on the pagan side, the same kind of thing."],[254.53,"Where you'd have them coming and saying, you know, okay, well, Christ is good, but you know, in addition to that, how about this."],[264.42,"Um, and again, to someone who had come from a background of paganism, different spiritual observances, the worship of different gods, different practices, and these things weren't mutually exclusive."],[278.58,"You could just sort of add both and."],[282.08,"Mix and mingle and and uh choose your own adventure, you know."],[286.52,"But uh this is why St."],[288.68,"Paul is hammered so much in his epistle to the Colossians on Christ being the fullness."],[289.88,"He's the whole thing"],[296.48,"There is no and after Christ."],[298.98,"There's no next level, there's no more, there's no if you have Christ, you have everything."],[304.02,"And he's been hammering that in over and over and over again."],[307.38,"Um we saw that especially in the first part before the therefore, where he was hammering in that Christ is the fullness, he's everything, he's the whole deal, all of God, all the fullness of deity in bodily form."],[320.77,"That's Christ. Um, and then we saw in sort of the second part after the therefore that we've been reading, him warning then about someone who's gonna come to you and claim they're gonna add something else, saying, Don't believe them."],[333.73,"And so now, as we pick up tonight, St"],[334.03,"You've already got everything, you don't need anything more."],[339.95,"Paul is going to move from that, uh, sort of segue from that into getting very, very practical about things in the life of the particular community that he's writing to."],[355.47,"Uh, as we made the point repeatedly in St."],[358.03,"Paul's epistles, uh, even the ones that sort of service quote unquote circular epistles where they were going to go to a bunch of communities, they're still directed to particular Christian communities at a particular time, dealing with actual issues and questions and things that are going on."],[375.23,"St. Paul isn't just sitting down and writing philosophical or theological treatises."],[380.41,"In a general sense."],[381.93,"Those things existed in the first century."],[384.73,"Philo wrote them, for example, in Alexandria."],[387.77,"St. Paul was educated enough, he could have written them."],[390.73,"If he did, we don't have them."],[392.51,"What we have are his letters to actual people."],[395.15,"And that's going to be hammered home probably tonight, toward the end, when he starts mentioning specific people, who are there, if there's any doubt."],[405.81,"But so unless there are any comments or questions or anything left over, we'll go ahead and pick up in St."],[412.69,"Paul's Epistle to the Colossians, chapter 3, verse 1."],[417.81,"If then you were raised with Christ, seek those things which are above. where Christ is, sitting at the right hand of God"],[428.65,"So St. Paul is here pivoting."],[433.05,"That's why we put the chapter break here."],[435.05,"This is a well placed one."],[436.57,"And there's this if then, which is sort of functioning a little bit like the therefore works."],[441.74,"So based on what I just said about you not needing anything else, other than Christ."],[448.45,"St. Paul is now pivoting to, so then what you need is more of Christ."],[453.43,"You need to come closer to Christ."],[455.19,"You need to move deeper in your knowledge of Christ, not trying to add something else."],[461.43,"And so that's why he says you need to set your mind on the things that are above."],[465.73,"And then quickly adds where Christ is."],[468.47,"Not above Christ."],[470.16,"Not some higher things in general, but the place where Christ is sitting at the right hand of God."],[476.48,"Set your mind on things above, not on things on the earth."],[480.72,"For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God."],[484.4,"When Christ, who is our life, appears, then you also will appear with him in glory."],[491.44,"So here's what's interesting here."],[493.92,"So St. Paul sort of reiterates in another way."],[497.92,"Set your mind on things above, not on things on the earth."],[501.3,"So you need to be focused on drawing closer to Christ, not on dealing with things on the earth."],[508.06,"How is that related to what he was just talking about?"],[510.7,"Well, most of the appeal of what these people would be offering would be related to things on earth."],[516.4,"So the way the pagan appeals to you is hey, are you worried about your crops this season?"],[521.26,"Or there's a temple of Demeter over here."],[523.02,"Why don't we go?"],[524.24,"Or uh, you know, if you come to this festival, you can get into this trade guild, and this will help your financial situation."],[535.57,"Um, all of these appeals are related to things on this earth, to reputation, to some kind of wisdom on this earth."],[541.59,"So St. Paul is saying, don't worry about that stuff, focus on Christ."],[546.05,"Those things will be taken care of."],[548.69,"But the reason he says the four. the because they're in verse three is he says, because you're dead"],[560.74,"So uh they probably weren't aware they were dead."],[564.4,"They thought they were still alive because the language he uses there of your life, being separate from your body."],[571.57,"This is physical death."],[572.87,"Your soul, your life separates from your body."],[576.81,"And so we're gonna see the next books we're gonna read are gonna be 1st and 2nd Thessalonians."],[581.37,"Say, Paul's gonna use this same kind of language about being hidden in Christ and appearing when Christ appears, about people who have died, about Christians who have died."],[592.09,"That this is what is happened to Christians who have died."],[593.13,"ing. Hidden mean we don't see it now"],[595.01,"Their life, their soul is hidden in Christ."],[599.81,"They're still alive, but we don't see it now."],[602.53,"And then when Christ returns, we'll be reunited."],[605.49,"That will be revealed."],[607.97,"But St. Paul is here applying this language to the people he's writing to who are reading the epistles."],[618.13,"And so he means this in two senses."],[623.33,"One of them is sort of an analogy, in the sense that dead people aren't worried about their bank balance."],[631.38,"Dead people aren't worried about the crops."],[634.02,"Dead people. So he's talking about not worrying about these things."],[638.06,"Um instead looking to Christ, waiting for Christ's return."],[644.0,"That's where you're gonna receive your reward."],[648.5,"This is something again, a lot of people want to separate what St."],[652.9,"Paul teaches from what Christ teaches, but Christ taught, especially in St."],[656.18,"Matthew's gospel over and over again, about uh reserving for yourself treasures in heaven rather than treasures on earth."],[663.78,"This was a constant thing."],[664.9,"Your reward will be great in heaven."],[666.96,"These people have received their reward already on this earth."],[670.41,"And St. Paul is really uh saying the same kind of thing here."],[674.46,"That that, you know, you spend your time worrying about the stuff here."],[678.3,"When in actuality your reward is sitting in Christ"],[682.71,"So that's what you should be focused on and worrying about."],[687.19,"Um, but he also means this in a certain way more literally, in the sense that we saw Saint Paul do this in Ephesians, in his epistle to the Ephesians, when he said that um uh we're already seated with Christ in the heavenly places."],[707.45,"That's post-Christ's return language that he's saying is already true of people who are alive on this earth."],[714.73,"So for Saint Paul, there's a point where we experience these things, but that's not the point where they become true."],[727.14,"Yes, I'm gonna explain more."],[729.54,"So, for example, to use another example."],[734.64,"Christ died and rose again at a particular point in history."],[739.49,"And when he did that, almost, you know, in my case, 1943 odd years before I was born."],[748.74,"Uh, he set me free from the power of death."],[752.28,"He did it."],[753.01,"He defeated the devil, he right uh established forgiveness of sins."],[759.09,"But there was a point in my life when I experienced that. when I came to know Christ"],[766.23,"It was all those things were still true before I came to experience them and know them."],[771.8,"But I experienced them at a certain point."],[775.32,"So Saint Paul often speaks about these things as realities because for him they are realities."],[783.9,"Different people will experience them at different times."],[787.36,"All of us, unless Christ returns first, are going to die physically."],[791.76,"And our soul, our life is gonna leave our body and it's gonna be hidden with Christ."],[796.25,"We're gonna experience that at different times."],[798.57,"But for St."],[799.13,"Paul, that's real all the time."],[803.01,"think And an I idea about time, which may or may not be correct."],[807.82,"That things that it's easier to talk about the past."],[812.46,"Things that have happened are eternally true."],[817.5,"They will always have happened."],[820.06,"Future the same way."],[821.42,"Things that are going to happen, it's already true that they will happen."],[827.08,"They don't know it."],[829.8,"The passage of time, what we're really talking about when we talk about time is the way humans experience time."],[837.69,"Not the way God does, not the way angels do, not the way demons do, not the way right."],[841.85,"So that's why I say Paul could also say that, you know, Christ is the lamb slain before the foundation of the world."],[849.64,"You know the crucifixion."],[851.18,"It it entered into human experience at a certain point in time."],[855.5,"Yeah. But it is eternally real and true."],[859.16,"Of course, Christ liberated those who were in Hades."],[863.88,"At a particular point, but also before that."],[867.25,"At a particular point in human experience."],[869.41,"And that different people experience that reality at different points."],[873.47,"Yeah. And so that's why St."],[875.79,"Paul can speak about this in this way."],[877.79,"And since it's true, he can make arguments based on it."],[882.77,"Just as in his epistle to the Ephesians, he said, because we're already seated and enthroned and ruling with Christ in the heavenly places, therefore, XYZ, he can now say, Hey, we're already dead."],[896.59,"We're already dead to this world."],[898.75,"We're awaiting right the return of Christ when we'll all be reunited, creation will be restored."],[904.85,"And therefore, don't worry about what's going on on earth, this petty stuff now."],[912.17,"Because we're waiting for that day."],[919.69,"Verse five, therefore."],[924.33,"So we have sort of a micro scale one here."],[927.07,"So we have that teaching."],[928.35,"Because this is true, therefore, put to death your members, which are on the earth."],[935.11,"Fornication, uncleanness, passion, evil desire, and covetousness, which is idolatry."],[943.83,"So a couple of things here."],[946.23,"So he says, Because you're dead, therefore kill these things."],[954.4,"But uh but we've seen him use this language before, like in his epistle to the Romans, you know, counting as dead to sin and alive in Christ."],[961.81,"Um but notice he refers to these as your members."],[966.37,"Yeah, that's what that was."],[967.61,"As your body parts."],[969.27,"And we talked about this back in trying to remember when we talked about it, probably in 1st and 2nd Corinthians."],[975.83,"We talked about body, the difference between body and meat."],[980.44,"That we think of body as being your meat, your physicality, your material, you know, uh body."],[987.56,"But body in the ancient world was more like a nexus of powers or potentialities."],[992.97,"And so members would be individual powers."],[998.23,"Individual potentialities. And so these are powers or potentialities that we have on this earth in this life."],[1008.37,"Fornication, uncleanness, passion."],[1010.37,"These are passions."],[1011.63,"These are things we could do."],[1012.83,"These are potentialities that we could make real."],[1015.07,"These are these are, you know, within our power to do."],[1018.59,"Those limbs, cut those off"],[1018.87,"And St. Paul is effectively saying, cut those off."],[1025.34,"Let them die."],[1027.87,"Kill those. And notice also that he identifies covetousness with idolatry."],[1035.49,"And that's for a couple of reasons."],[1039.41,"One is just to make the point of how serious it is."],[1042.29,"Because remember, sexual immorality and idolatry are the two worst things you can do."],[1047.35,"For the perspective of the Torah."],[1049.67,"Maybe murder is up there very close."],[1052.52,"But uh those are really bad."],[1055.26,"And so covetousness is usually the thing that uh of the Ten Commandments, certainly, the thing that we think of as the least serious."],[1063.44,"Looking at a catalog a little too long, you know, sort of imagining what I'm gonna do, you know, when I uh win the publisher's clearing house sweepstakes."],[1073.04,"Uh you know, th those are the that seems to be sort of the most harmless one."],[1078.48,"That's certainly not as bad as adultery or stealing or murder or you know, dishonoring your your parents."],[1086.26,"All those seem worse."],[1087.38,"This seems like the least one."],[1088.74,"St. Paul here is pointing is comparing it to the most serious one."],[1094.04,"And and what is the connection?"],[1095.56,"Well, the connection is based on what he just said."],[1100.37,"You're you're you're looking at things on earth."],[1102.55,"You're coveting things on earth."],[1103.59,"Where Christ is"],[1104.03,"You're not coveting things above."],[1107.09,"You're coveting things on this earth, which means you're focusing your consciousness, your mind, your attention on those rather than Christ."],[1117.11,"Instead of Christ, which is what antichrist literally means."],[1121.53,"And so that's how it's connected to idolatry."],[1125.06,"Going after sort of material possessions and things and your earthly well-being is not different for one of these people in Colossae than going to one of the pagan temples or one of the pagan festivals."],[1140.6,"It's not, it's not somehow better because there aren't demons directly involved."],[1145.58,"Mammon is still a deal."],[1147.82,"This is especially timely for us because, of course, we want to play pretend that there aren't any demons around, and none of those pagan gods exist anymore, and nobody worships them."],[1159.42,"But the way we act toward material things and our earthly well-being is awful similar."],[1168.6,"To the way the pagans acted."],[1170.68,"They just were more honest."],[1181.56,"So that's probably qualifies as consciousness."],[1185.56,"Yeah. It's because for St."],[1187.54,"Paul here, it's a question of focus."],[1190.46,"Where is your thought, where are your thoughts focused?"],[1192.94,"Things above where Christ is, or things down here."],[1196.78,"Yeah. Verse 6, because of these things, those passions he just mentioned, the wrath of God is coming upon the sons of disobedience, in which you yourselves once walked when you lived in them."],[1214.82,"Okay, so because of these things, wrath of God is coming on sons of disobedience."],[1220.9,"Sons of is uh a basic sort of Semitic phrase that means you possess that quality."],[1230.66,"I just read that in a book."],[1232.5,"Oh. I've got another thought"],[1236.54,"Yeah, so Barnabas, means son of encouragement."],[1239.86,"That means he is someone who possesses this this quality."],[1243.54,"And so here, sons of disobedience means these are the disobedient ones."],[1249.14,"These are the people who are fully exercising these members."],[1253.32,"To continue the analogy, who are pursuing the fornication, the uncleanness, the passion, the evil desire, the covetousness."],[1260.46,"The wrath of God is coming upon them."],[1262.54,"But notice St."],[1263.5,"Paul is always very quick to point out."],[1265.91,"This isn't that you're better than them."],[1267.61,"Because he says, Because in which you yourselves once walked."],[1272.23,"These are you guys."],[1274.13,"When you lived that way. you were in the same position they were"],[1277.83,"So it's not that you're better, but it's that now that you have Christ, you need to walk differently."],[1284.95,"He's been using that walk language."],[1287.11,"Is he assuming that any pagan would have been doing these things simply because they were pagans?"],[1298.47,"I don't know that it's an assumption in the Roman Empire."],[1306.55,"It is simply true."],[1310.31,"I mean, uh at this period in time, you know, there were established social institutions that were completely, for example, sexually immoral from a Christian perspective."],[1322.56,"Um you have stoics around who are still held off as the immoral we talk about in the last week."],[1332.0,"Yeah. Um but then you've got the idolatry, you've got the covetousness, you've got the uncleanness, you've got the right."],[1341.04,"Um, so he's not saying that that every single pagan is a hideous monster and the worst they could possibly be."],[1350.82,"But he's saying uh if we compare these members to muscles you're exercising, they're exercising all the wrong muscles."],[1358.9,"You need to cut them off."],[1362.48,"Not just stop using them so much, but dead, gone."],[1367.85,"Because this is where that leads."],[1371.07,"This is where that's leading people, this is where it was leading you."],[1374.92,"When you were still living that way, that's why you need to stop completely, zero, none."],[1380.73,"So uh St."],[1382.17,"Paul isn't primarily talking about them."],[1385.79,"This isn't about St."],[1386.59,"Paul condemning them."],[1388.35,"This is about St."],[1389.23,"Paul warning the the uh members of the Christian community in Colossae that they have to stop."],[1398.19,"There has to be a break, there has to be a radical break and change in how they live."],[1408.95,"Verse 8, he continues, but now you yourselves are to put off all these anger, wrath, malice, blasphemy, filthy language out of your mouth."],[1419.83,"Um the difference between anger anger and wrath, anger is sort of what we think of as anger like frustration."],[1425.19,"Wrath is more related to like vengeance."],[1427.91,"Like you've injured or harmed me or someone I care about, so I'm going to now go and take exact exact a toll, yes."],[1437.75,"Um malice and ill will."],[1440.89,"Blasphemy, filthy language."],[1442.87,"So he's saying he's getting even more practical."],[1445.96,"So it's not just these ab traits."],[1448.32,"Here's specifics. Zero tolerance, gone"],[1450.1,"All these things."],[1455.46,"Verse 9, do not lie to one another, since you have put off the old man with his deeds."],[1461.14,"Okay. So the old man is not picking on elderly males."],[1465.92,"It's the old man is in that body you used to have that person you used to be."],[1470.56,"Former man. Yeah, yeah."],[1472.0,"Who used to be."],[1473.04,"You've put it off."],[1474.16,"And so that again, you can't keep doing the things that you used to do."],[1482.64,"Verse 10, and have put on the new man who is renewed in knowledge according to the image of him who created him."],[1489.2,"So this again."],[1491.28,"Remember, he's talked before already in the epistle about the knowledge. of Christ"],[1495.64,"Having the knowledge of Christ, meaning knowing Christ."],[1498.94,"Being this transformative thing, and he brings that up again here."],[1502.24,"So the person you are now, the body you have now, has been transformed."],[1507.69,"Because you've come to know Christ."],[1510.43,"And made new."],[1511.95,"That renewed been made new again."],[1517.39,"In the image of him who created him."],[1519.47,"So speaking. Where there is neither Greek nor Jew, circumcised nor uncircumcised, barbarian, Scythian, slave nor free, but Christ is all and in all."],[1533.41,"Is this a famous passage that says that or is there another one?"],[1537.77,"There's another one."],[1538.41,"Where's that you're thinking of?"],[1541.21,"And I'm trying to remember I think it's in First Corinthians."],[1548.49,"Yeah, this one doesn't."],[1549.85,"So I'll look in first."],[1551.29,"Yeah. Um so these are uh distinctions in what they saw at the time uh as uh the quality of a human being."],[1569.45,"We are horribly averse and it's not necessarily a bad thing, it comes out of Christianity."],[1575.61,"Uh we've been influenced by this passage even even if perhaps subconsciously with many people but it was taken for granted in the ancient world and for much of Earth's subsequent history in places untouched by Christianity that human beings are of varying qualities um I mean Plato straight out argues this in the Republic you know yeah and so uh some people some classes of people are just better than other classes of people."],[1612.57,"Now, this isn't he doesn't primarily talk about economic class here."],[1617.61,"Slave nor free is pretty close to that, but um but yes, so in in in the Roman world, a free male Roman citizen was uh the most truly human person, was legally persona, was legally a person."],[1636.09,"And then you go down from there."],[1637.87,"Next you have free female Roman person."],[1642.85,"And then you have uh who's a citizen."],[1646.3,"Then you have uh free non citizens, who are Roman."],[1651.33,"Then you have uh free non-citizens."],[1656.15,"And eventually, and male above female and all of those."],[1659.61,"Um, and then you get down to slaves down at the bottom, and you probably have some people haggle over which was worse, a slave or a barbarian."],[1669.89,"Because at least slaves are sort of Roman."],[1673.01,"But and this kind of thing, you see this in India's caste system."],[1677.33,"You see this in you know, anywhere where paganism has existed."],[1682.35,"Um, and these are justified in various ways, they're justified religiously within Hindu thought."],[1689.15,"Uh they're justified that hasn't at least in the past have a class system as very no, no, there's no place that hasn't."],[1698.43,"Um maybe when we were hunter-gatherers."],[1703.79,"You had a chief and then you had everybody else."],[1706.59,"But um, yeah, and or or these things are naturalized."],[1711.15,"Um that's been more common more recently"],[1715.73,"But you get a little bit of it in the ancient world where they were starting to do science, where they come up with sort of pseudoscientific or quasi-scientific reasons why this group is superior to that other group."],[1727.81,"Uh you get people measuring skulls."],[1730.13,"Yeah. Measuring skulls and that kind of thing."],[1732.69,"Um that kind of pseudoscience."],[1735.09,"But so Saint Paul uh is firing the first shot on that."],[1741.78,"Uh that anyone who has come to the knowledge of Christ has been transformed and renewed."],[1747.64,"You're now, after like I said, the new man, the new humanity."],[1752.2,"This is not a new man."],[1753.72,"This is the new humanity."],[1756.41,"So humanity was created, in the garden."],[1762.51,"Or was created actually technically outside the garden, put the garden."],[1765.79,"But you know, Adam is created, Eve is created from Adam, but Adam is split."],[1771.87,"And so humanity is something."],[1774.67,"Humanity through disobedience becomes something else."],[1778.67,"And so whatever of these supposedly qualitative distinctions people have wanted to make in this world among fallen humanity are for St."],[1796.35,"Paul eradicated in in the new humanity, which comes into being through, as we saw earlier the epistle baptism, through the knowledge of Christ, through being remade in the image of Christ, because it's Christ's humanity."],[1811.27,"It's Christ's human nature, which has been deified, which we receive in Christ as Christians."],[1819.53,"And so if you are a Greek and you come to know Christ, and you're baptized, you become part of the church, you have that human new human nature."],[1830.43,"If you're a Jew, same thing."],[1832.83,"If you're circumcised, same thing."],[1835.47,"If you're uncircumcised, same thing."],[1837.63,"If you're a barbarian or a Scythian, same thing."],[1842.31,"If you're a slave, same thing."],[1844.79,"If you're free, same thing."],[1848.47,"Because Christ is all in all."],[1850.47,"You're all now remade in the image of Christ."],[1854.39,"And you're sharing in Christ's humanity."],[1856.31,"The same way you used to share in Adam's humanity."],[1859.75,"As uh St."],[1860.63,"Paul said in Acts 17, from one man he made all the nations, of the world, about God."],[1866.89,"Now Christ is making people from all nations, from all nations new."],[1874.17,"And so I'll go ahead and touch on it here because we're gonna get St."],[1879.77,"Paul's gonna talk about slaves again here in just a little bit, and that's the bit that always gets quoted to try to say that St."],[1887.89,"Paul is pro-slavery."],[1889.73,"But what he has just said here has completely undermined and destroyed the institution of slavery."],[1895.89,"Because the institution of slavery in all of its incarnations in every part of the world has always been based on the idea that the slave is inferior qualitatively, is inferior as a human to whoever the master is."],[1916.53,"That's always what it's been predicated on."],[1919.17,"They've used all kinds of different arguments, for how they're inferior or how the other is superior, and why, and why this is natural, or why this is good, or why this is right, but that's the basis."],[1931.02,"Yeah And so St."],[1936.18,"Yeah. That's inferior."],[1939.46,"Paul has here undermined that."],[1943.54,"Because if someone who owns other people, claims to own other people, is baptized, and they're also baptized, he is no longer superior to them."],[1954.9,"They are the same."],[1957.3,"And therefore, there is no possible justification for his claim of quote unquote ownership."],[1963.3,"They're now brothers."],[1965.56,"And no Roman would enslave a member of his own family."],[1970.76,"That would be the greatest dishonor and shame imaginable."],[1974.36,"And then the Potter Famelius could kill his children."],[1975.88,"No, not his brothers"],[1978.08,"His children, but not his brother."],[1983.84,"Because they're equals."],[1985.3,"The reason the Potter Familius could could do that with his children was there is inferiors."],[1990.6,"Because he's the father."],[1991.9,"But so if you're brothers, if you're the same."],[1996.66,"Fratricise right out."],[2002.26,"Verse twelve. Therefore, as elective God, notice they added the word the."],[2009.38,"I didn't notice that."],[2012.9,"There's a slight theological bent to that."],[2016.0,"Uh there's a difference between saying therefore, as chosen by God."],[2021.73,"Is very different than saying, therefore, as the elective God, as part of the group of those who have been chosen by God."],[2028.53,"Those are very different statements."],[2029.81,"So let's drop the the since it's not actually there."],[2033.81,"Therefore, as elective God, holy and beloved."],[2037.99,"So he's saying these are the things that are true of you."],[2040.97,"So he said, these things, he started with the negative."],[2044.33,"These things are no longer true of you."],[2046.57,"So put them off."],[2047.61,"Get rid of them."],[2048.58,"Throw them off."],[2049.46,"Now he's gonna say put on."],[2051.24,"So now this is the positive."],[2052.28,"Here are the things that are true of you."],[2055.53,"Elect of God meaning chosen by God."],[2059.69,"God loves you."],[2060.41,"you. He's made holy"],[2063.23,"That's the elected beloved."],[2067.04,"Like when Christ says to his disciples, you didn't choose me, I chose you."],[2070.88,"This isn't some Calvinist salvation thing."],[2075.04,"This is Jesus went and called his disciples."],[2098.8,"Christ sent people like St."],[2100.72,"Paul out to find them."],[2102.88,"Right when he says chosen"],[2103.1,"That's what he's pointing to."],[2107.5,"That it's it's not just that, hey, you found the secret."],[2112.14,"You found the recipe for salvation and eternal life."],[2115.44,"No, Christ loved you, he died for you, he came and he sought you and he found you."],[2121.14,"And there's no implication here that there's other people who that's not true of."],[2127.54,"I would guess the implication is that there are other people that are going to be."],[2132.42,"Yes, who are, yes."],[2133.86,"But that this is true of everyone."],[2136.52,"Who whom St."],[2137.72,"Paul is talking to."],[2140.92,"And so because this is true of you."],[2143.89,"Then you need to put on t.ender mercies, kindness, humility, meekness, long suffering, bearing with one another, forgiving one another"],[2153.01,"If anyone has a complaint against another, even as Christ forgave you, so you also."],[2159.17,"Then again, must do, it's added, but implied."],[2163.09,"This epic must have seemed quite shocking to Romans."],[2168.37,"Yes, this was bizarre."],[2169.73,"Yeah, I mean, they would have objected to it the same way Dietzie objected to it."],[2174.69,"Yes, yes. This is slave morality."],[2176.93,"Yes. This is, you know, first of all, coming out and saying slaves are just as good as masters."],[2181.33,"L likeike, who are you kidding?"],[2183.91,"But um meekness was not."],[2186.31,"And that was also a lot of why the Romans dismissed Christianity early on."],[2189.51,"They're like, it's all women and slaves."],[2191.79,"What kind of dumb religion is that?"],[2193.97,"That was their their thinking because of their their uh aristocratic sort of mindset."],[2200.77,"Um but yeah, so this is this is bizarre world."],[2205.99,"And we talked about this before a little bit, uh that this is embedded really in the family language that's always getting used about the church of brothers and sisters and family."],[2215.69,"So the Romans understood, like we were just saying, that your family members, yes, you have certain obligations and duties to them with honor and shame attached to it."],[2227.89,"But Christians extended that to like strangers and just other people of the community. and like slaves and barbarians and just whoever"],[2238.73,"That made that made zero sense."],[2242.01,"That made zero sense."],[2243.45,"And I'll add, if we actually practiced this better in the church, it would be bizarro and strange to our culture."],[2253.31,"In in the United States, as Christian as it may want to claim to be sometimes."],[2260.59,"Yeah, this would this would be absolutely bizarre."],[2263.07,"And even back when they were making that claim, it would have looked bizarre to actually do it."],[2268.75,"Yes, they would have thought you were a hippie commune."],[2272.75,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2280.07,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."],[2285.43,"That's wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.4,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:1 - Catch-up: mirror reading and \"more\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Colossians 3:1</b>; intro is on the first Colossians study</li><li class=\"d0\">Many hunt for a \"<b>Colossian heresy</b>\" by <b>mirror reading</b></li><li class=\"d1\">look at what Paul preaches against → \"they believed XYZ\" → people \"tie themselves in knots\" to make it one group</li><li class=\"d0\">No reason for <b>one particular group</b> - Paul talks about <b>different beliefs</b>, never a group</li><li class=\"d1\">last time: things applying to <b>former pagans</b> - pagan pursuit of <b>wisdom, mysteries, mystery cults</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also warning of <b>Jewish parties</b> arriving to get them <b>circumcised</b>, following Torah commandments in the old covenant sense, imposed on <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the same group - \"takes a lot of <b>special pleading</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":147.66,"lv":3,"h":"What unites the warnings: another level","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Unifying idea for <b>former pagans</b> (most in Colossae, Laodicea)</li><li class=\"d1\">first-century pagan religion: always <b>another level, step, new mystery</b>, new god, cult, festival</li><li class=\"d1\">could \"keep going deeper and deeper\" - <b>experiential mode</b>: \"now a new deeper experience of XYZ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>spirituality</b> they were used to</li><li class=\"d0\">Different groups with different ideas could <b>appeal on that level</b> - \"the next step\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Judaizers</b>: next step = <b>circumcision</b>, <b>food laws</b>, <b>Jewish feast days</b> in addition to Christian observances</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan side: \"Christ is good, but <b>in addition</b>, how about this?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>mutually exclusive</b> for pagans - \"mix and mingle\", \"<b>choose your own adventure</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":286.52,"lv":3,"h":"Christ the fullness: no \"and\" after Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why Paul <b>hammers</b> Christ as the <b>fullness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>no 'and' after Christ</b>\" - no next level, no more - have Christ, have <b>everything</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first part (before the <b>therefore</b>): Christ the whole deal, <b>all the fullness of deity in bodily form</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second part: warning about <b>anyone adding something</b>, \"you already have everything\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tonight: segue into getting \"very, very <b>practical</b>\" about the <b>life of the community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even \"circular\" epistles go to <b>particular communities</b> at a particular time, with <b>actual issues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not general philosophical/theological <b>treatises</b> - those existed (<b>Philo</b> in <b>Alexandria</b>); Paul educated enough, \"if he did, we don't have them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what we have = <b>letters to actual people</b> - hammered home toward the end with <b>specific names</b></li></ul>"},{"t":412.69,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:1-2 - Seek the things above","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1</b>: \"If then you were <b>raised with Christ</b>, seek those things which are <b>above where Christ is</b>, sitting at the right hand of God\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pivot</b> - well-placed chapter break; \"<b>if then</b>\" works like the <b>therefore</b></li><li class=\"d1\">based on not needing anything else → what you need is <b>more of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">come closer, <b>deeper in knowledge of Christ</b>, not add something</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:2</b>: set your mind on things above, <b>not on things on the earth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"where Christ is\" - <b>not above Christ</b>, not \"some higher things in general\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Link to what came before: appeal of these people = <b>things on earth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crops: temple of <b>Demeter</b>; festival → trade guild → <b>finances</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>reputation</b>, wisdom on this earth</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: don't worry about that - focus on Christ - \"those things will be taken care of\"</li></ul>"},{"t":548.69,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:3-4 - You died; life hidden with Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:3-4</b>: \"you <b>died</b>, and your life is <b>hidden with Christ in God</b>. When Christ, who is our life, <b>appears</b>, then you also will <b>appear</b> with him in glory\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Because you're <b>dead</b>\" - they probably <b>weren't aware</b> they were dead</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>life separate from body</b> = physical death - soul/life separates from body</li><li class=\"d0\">Next books: <b>1 and 2 Thessalonians</b> - same language of being <b>hidden in Christ</b> until he appears, about <b>Christians who have died</b></li><li class=\"d1\">their soul is <b>hidden in Christ</b> - still alive, \"we don't see it now\"; reunited when Christ returns</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul applies it to <b>the living readers</b>, two ways</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>analogy</b>: dead people aren't worried about <b>bank balance or crops</b> → look to Christ, wait for his return, <b>reward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ taught the same, esp. <b>Matthew</b>: <b>treasures in heaven</b> vs earth; those who received reward on earth</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul = Christ's teaching - not to be separated</li></ul>"},{"t":687.19,"lv":3,"h":"More literally: already true, experienced later","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>more literal</b>: <b>Ephesians</b> - <b>already seated with Christ in the heavenly places</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"post-Christ's-return language\" already true of the living</li><li class=\"d0\">A point where we <b>experience</b> these things ≠ the point they <b>become true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ died and rose at a point in history (~1943 years before I was born) → <b>freed me from death's power</b>, defeated the devil, forgiveness of sins</li><li class=\"d1\">I experienced it when I <b>came to know Christ</b> - true before I experienced it</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul speaks of them as <b>realities</b> - they are</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone experiences at <b>different times</b>: unless Christ returns first all die - soul <b>hidden with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Idea about time (maybe not correct): past things are <b>eternally true</b> - \"will always have happened\"; future too</li><li class=\"d1\">time = the way <b>humans experience time</b> - not God, angels, demons</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ the <b>lamb slain before the foundation of the world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crucifixion entered <b>human experience</b> at a point, but <b>eternally real</b>; liberated those in <b>Hades</b> at a point, but also before</li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul can <b>argue from it</b>: as in Ephesians \"already seated and enthroned... therefore XYZ\" → here \"<b>already dead</b> to this world, awaiting Christ's return, creation restored → <b>don't worry about petty stuff</b> now\"</li></ul>"},{"t":919.69,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:5 - Put to death your members","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:5</b>: \"Therefore <b>put to death your members</b> which are on the earth: <b>fornication, uncleanness, passion, evil desire, and covetousness, which is idolatry</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Because you're dead, therefore <b>kill these things</b>\" - cf. <b>Romans</b>: <b>count yourselves dead to sin</b>, alive in Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Members</b> = <b>body parts</b> - body vs <b>meat</b> (earlier, 1-2 Corinthians)</li><li class=\"d1\">body in the ancient world = <b>nexus of powers or potentialities</b>; members = <b>individual powers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">potentialities we could <b>make real</b> - \"within our power to do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"<b>cut those off</b>... let them die\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1027.87,"lv":3,"h":"Covetousness is idolatry","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why identify <b>covetousness with idolatry</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">one: shows <b>how serious</b> - <b>sexual immorality and idolatry</b> the two worst things from the Torah's view; murder close</li><li class=\"d1\">covetousness seems the <b>least serious</b> of the Ten Commandments - \"looking at a catalog a little too long\", <b>Publishers Clearing House</b> daydreams</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul compares it to the <b>most serious</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connection: coveting <b>things on earth</b>, not things above where Christ is</li><li class=\"d1\">focusing consciousness, mind, attention on those <b>instead of Christ</b> = literally <b>anti-Christ</b> → idolatry</li><li class=\"d0\">Chasing possessions and earthly well-being = <b>same as pagan temple or festival</b> - not better \"because there aren't demons directly involved\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Mammon</b> still</li><li class=\"d1\">timely: we \"play pretend\" no demons/pagan gods - but our acting toward <b>material things</b> is \"awful similar\" - pagans \"more honest\"</li><li class=\"d0\">For Paul a question of <b>focus</b>: where are your thoughts - above where Christ is, or down here</li></ul>"},{"t":1196.78,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:6-7 - The wrath coming on sons of disobedience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:6</b>: \"because of these things the <b>wrath of God</b> is coming upon the <b>sons of disobedience</b>, in which you yourselves <b>once walked</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sons of</b> = Semitic phrase: you <b>possess that quality</b> - e.g. <b>Barnabas</b> = son of encouragement</li><li class=\"d1\">sons of disobedience = <b>the disobedient ones</b> fully exercising these members</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul quick to point out: <b>not that you're better</b> - \"you yourselves once walked\" in the same position</li><li class=\"d1\">now that you have Christ → <b>walk differently</b> (\"walk\" language)</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: assumes pagans did these things? A: \"simply true\" in the Roman Empire - established <b>sexually immoral</b> social institutions, idolatry, covetousness</li><li class=\"d1\">not every pagan a \"hideous monster\" (cf. <b>Stoics</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">members as <b>muscles</b>: \"exercising all the wrong muscles\" → cut off, \"<b>dead, gone</b>\", not just use less</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is where it was <b>leading you</b>\" → stop completely, \"<b>zero, none</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not Paul condemning them - <b>warning the Christians</b> of Colossae: a <b>radical break</b> in how they live</li></ul>"},{"t":1408.95,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:8-10 - Put off the old, put on the new","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:8</b>: \"put off all these: <b>anger, wrath, malice, blasphemy, filthy language</b> out of your mouth\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>anger</b> = frustration; <b>wrath</b> = <b>vengeance</b> - \"you've harmed me, I'll exact a toll\"</li><li class=\"d1\">more <b>practical</b> - specifics; \"<b>zero tolerance</b>, gone\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:9</b>: \"do not lie to one another, since you have <b>put off the old man</b> with his deeds\"</li><li class=\"d1\">old man ≠ \"picking on elderly males\" - the <b>person you used to be</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:10</b>: \"put on the <b>new man</b> who is <b>renewed in knowledge</b> according to the image of him who created him\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>knowledge of Christ</b> (earlier in epistle) = knowing Christ, <b>transformative</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the person/body you now are <b>transformed</b> because you've come to know Christ - in the <b>image of Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1519.47,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:11 - Neither Greek nor Jew","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:11</b>: \"neither <b>Greek nor Jew, circumcised nor uncircumcised, barbarian, Scythian, slave nor free</b>, but <b>Christ is all and in all</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: similar famous passage? A: <b>1 Corinthians</b> includes <b>male and female</b> - this one doesn't</li><li class=\"d0\">Distinctions in <b>quality of a human being</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we're \"horribly averse\" to that - from <b>Christianity</b>, influenced by this passage, even subconsciously</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient world (and places untouched by Christianity): humans of <b>varying qualities</b> - <b>Plato</b> in the <b>Republic</b>; some classes just better</li><li class=\"d0\">Not primarily economic class; Roman ranking: <b>free male Roman citizen</b> = most truly human, legally a <b>persona</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then free female citizen, free non-citizens, male above female, <b>slaves</b> at bottom</li><li class=\"d1\">haggling: slave vs <b>barbarian</b> - slaves at least \"sort of Roman\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>India's caste system</b>, \"anywhere paganism has existed\", justified <b>religiously</b> (Hindu thought)</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe not hunter-gatherers - chief and everybody else</li><li class=\"d1\">later <b>naturalized</b>: pseudoscience, <b>measuring skulls</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1735.09,"lv":3,"h":"The new humanity and Christ's human nature","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul \"<b>firing the first shot</b>\" on that</li><li class=\"d0\">Anyone who has come to know Christ is <b>transformed, renewed</b> - not a new man, \"the <b>new humanity</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam created, Eve from Adam; humanity through <b>disobedience</b> becomes something else; <b>in Christ</b> becomes something else again</li><li class=\"d1\">supposed <b>qualitative distinctions</b> among fallen humanity → <b>eradicated</b> in the new humanity</li><li class=\"d1\">via <b>baptism</b>, knowledge of Christ, remade in his image - it's <b>Christ's human nature, deified</b>, which we receive</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek / Jew / circumcised / uncircumcised / barbarian / Scythian / slave / free: <b>same thing</b> - \"Christ is all in all\"</li><li class=\"d1\">share in <b>Christ's humanity</b> as we shared in <b>Adam's</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Acts 17</b>: \"from <b>one man</b> he made all the nations\" → now Christ making <b>new people from all nations</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1874.17,"lv":3,"h":"This undermines slavery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Touching it now since Paul will <b>talk about slaves again</b> soon - the bit always quoted to say Paul is <b>pro-slavery</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What he just said \"<b>completely undermined and destroyed</b> the institution of slavery\"</li><li class=\"d1\">slavery everywhere based on the idea the slave is <b>qualitatively inferior</b> as a human to the master</li><li class=\"d1\">arguments vary: natural, good, right, <b>captives in war</b> \"lost\" = inferior</li><li class=\"d0\">If the owner and the owned are both baptized → he's <b>no longer superior</b> - \"they are the same\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no justification</b> for claim of \"ownership\" → now <b>brothers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no Roman would <b>enslave a family member</b> - greatest dishonor and shame</li><li class=\"d1\">the <i>pater familias</i> could kill his <b>children</b>, but <b>not his brother</b> - children = inferiors because he's the father; brothers equal → \"fratricide right out\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2002.26,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 3:12 - Elect of God, holy and beloved","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:12</b>: \"Therefore, as <b>elect of God</b>, holy and beloved\" - translation adds \"<b>the</b>\" - \"slight theological bent\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"chosen by God\" ≠ \"<b>the elect</b> of God\" (part of a group chosen)</li><li class=\"d0\">Started with the negative: <b>put off</b>; now the positive - <b>put on</b>; what <b>is</b> true of you</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Elect</b> = chosen; <b>God loves you</b>, made you <b>holy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you didn't choose me, I <b>chose you</b>\" - \"not some <b>Calvinist</b> salvation thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christians in Colossae didn't go <b>looking</b> for the Most High God - Christ <b>sent people like Paul</b> to find them</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"you found the secret recipe\" - \"Christ <b>loved</b> you, <b>died</b> for you, <b>sought</b> you and found you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no implication others excluded - others are to be reached; true of <b>everyone Paul is talking to</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2140.92,"lv":3,"h":"Colossians 3:12-13 - Mercies, forgiveness, and the Roman reaction","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Therefore <b>put on</b>: \"tender mercies, kindness, humility, meekness, long-suffering, <b>bearing with one another, forgiving one another</b>\" - \"even as <b>Christ forgave you</b>, so you also\" (\"must do\" added, but implied)</li><li class=\"d0\">Ethic <b>shocking to Romans</b> - <b>Nietzsche</b> objected: \"<b>slave morality</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"slaves just as good as masters - who are you kidding?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>meekness</b> not a virtue; Romans dismissed early Christianity: \"<b>all women and slaves</b>\" - aristocratic mindset</li><li class=\"d0\">Embedded in the <b>family language</b> - church of <b>brothers and sisters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Romans: family has <b>obligations and duties</b>, honor and shame</li><li class=\"d1\">Christians extended it to <b>strangers</b>, slaves, barbarians, \"whoever\" → \"<b>zero sense</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If we practiced it better in the church, it'd be \"<b>bizarro</b>\" in the United States - even back when it claimed to be Christian</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they'd think you were a <b>hippie commune</b>\"</li></ul>"}]});
